::: {.doc-subject}

Add a prism graph to the current graph.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L8521-L8656){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}
```pythonsignature
Graph.add_prism_graph(
    n,
    type=None,
    label=True,
    rel=None,
)
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

![Prism graph](../assets/graphs/add-prism-graph.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt="Three prism graphs side by side for n=3, 4, and 5, each drawn as an outer n-cycle around an inner n-cycle with n rungs joining matching nodes. That gives 2n nodes and 3n edges: 6 and 9, 8 and 12, 10 and 15." width="100%"}

Creates two parallel n-cycles connected by n vertical edges, forming a 3D prism structure
when visualized. The result has `2n` nodes and `3n` edges (`n` per cycle plus `n`
"rungs"), and every node has degree `3`. With `n=3` this is the triangular prism and with
`n=4` it is the cube graph.

The "top" cycle is created first, then the "bottom" cycle, and the rungs join the `i`-th
top node to the `i`-th bottom node. In a directed graph each cycle runs in one direction
(from each node to the next) and every rung points from top to bottom. The prism is
appended to the graph without touching or connecting to existing nodes.

Prism graphs are a compact example of a 3-regular (cubic) graph, which makes them useful
for:

- testing algorithms on regular, symmetric graphs where all nodes look alike
- modeling ring-shaped networks with a redundant parallel ring (e.g. dual-ring
  topologies)
- illustrative examples of planar, Hamiltonian, polyhedral graphs
:::

## Parameters {.doc-parameters}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code>[n]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [int]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   Number of nodes in each cycle. Must be at least `3`; the graph will have `2n` nodes.

<code>[type]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Optional[str]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Optional value stored in the `type` attribute of every new node. If `None` (default),
    no `type` attribute is set.

<code>[label]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[True]{.va}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   If `True` (default), nodes are labelled with a number plus a suffix marking their
    cycle: `"1_top"` to `"n_top"`, then `"(n+1)_bottom"` to `"(2n)_bottom"` (e.g.
    `"5_bottom"` for `n=4`). The number is the node's ID, and a label that is already used
    raises `ValueError` before anything is added. If `False`, nodes are created without
    labels.

<code>[rel]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Optional[str]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Optional value stored in the `rel` attribute of every new edge (cycle edges and rungs
    alike). If `None` (default), no `rel` attribute is set.
:::

## Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [Graph]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   Self for method chaining. The graph is modified in place.
:::

## Raises {.doc-raises}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   If `n=` is less than `3`.
:::

## See Also {.doc-see-also}

[](`~add_ladder_graph`) 

:   Add a ladder graph.

[](`~add_generalized_petersen_graph`) 

:   Add a generalized Petersen graph.

[](`~add_hypercube_graph`) 

:   Add a hypercube graph.

## Examples {.doc-examples}

A prism graph is two `n`-cycles, a top and a bottom, with a rung from each node of the top
to the matching node of the bottom. It has `2n` nodes and `3n` edges, and every node has
three neighbors:

::: {#b45e9a25 .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import edgecase as ec

graph = ec.Graph().add_prism_graph(n=5)

graph.count_nodes(), graph.count_edges(), graph.is_k_regular(3)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}
```
(10, 15, True)
```
:::
:::


The labels say which cycle each node is on. With `n=4`, the prism is the cube:

::: {#2e4c6073 .cell execution_count=2}
``` {.python .cell-code}
cube = ec.Graph().add_prism_graph(n=4)

(
    cube.get_node_df()["label"].to_list(),
    cube.is_isomorphic(ec.Graph().add_hypercube_graph(3)),
)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}
```
